# Source(s) & Extraction

**I've attached the csv's I used for this project. The site allows you to download the whole zip file to see other csv's not used for this analysis.**

**SITE: https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/scf/dataviz/scf/chart/#series:Education_Installment_Loans;demographic:all;population:1;units:median;range:1989,2022**

The data to complete this project is from the Federal Reserve Board's Survey of Consumer Finances (SCF), a survey conducted every three years that collects financial information from
American households including income, assets and debt, to name a few. Households are selected through a sampling process and are interviewed directly. The households interviewed self
report their net worth and loan amounts, as well as other financial assets but not viewed in this analysis. The SCF has been conducting surveys since 1989 and the specific csv's used 
in the project looks at the median of financial values with four specific demographic groups: age group, income category, education level, and race/ethnicity from 1989 to 2022. Below 
are the 9 variables from each demographic groups used to merge together. 

year: What year the survey was done (Numeric; int)  
group_label: The demographic group label (Text; string) 
Before_Tax_Income: The median household income (pre-tax) (Numeric; float) 
Net_Worth: Median total assets minus total debts (Numeric; float) 
Retirement_Accounts: Median retirement account balance (Numeric; float) 
Transaction_Accounts: Median checking and saving accounts balance (Numeric; float) 
Debt: Median total household debt (Numeric; float) 
Education_Installment_Loans: Median total education loan balance (Numeric; float) 
Credit_Card_Balances: Median total credit card debt (Numeric; float) 

```{python}
# loading age group data from the Federal Reserve Survey of Consumer Finances (SCF)
age = pd.read_csv("interactive_bulletin_charts_agecl_median.csv")
# checking column names and the first and last few rows 
age.columns
age.head()
age.tail()
# similar to age group, but now for education level data
education = pd.read_csv("interactive_bulletin_charts_edcl_median.csv")
# checking column names and the first and last few rows 
education.columns
education.head()
education.tail()
# now loading income category data
income = pd.read_csv("interactive_bulletin_charts_inccat_median.csv")
# checking column names and the first and last few rows 
income.columns
income.head()
income.tail()
# finally, loading in race and ethnicity data
race = pd.read_csv("interactive_bulletin_charts_racecl4_median.csv")
# checking column names and the first and last few rows
race.columns
race.head()
race.tail()
# selecting the columns I need from each dataframe and that are relevant to the research question
columns_needed = ["year", "Category", "Before_Tax_Income", "Net_Worth", "Retirement_Accounts", "Transaction_Accounts", "Debt", "Education_Installment_Loans", "Credit_Card_Balances"]
# using the above "columns_needed" variable and applying it to each dataframe to find what i'm looking for
age = age[columns_needed]
education = education[columns_needed]
income = income[columns_needed]
race = race[columns_needed]
# renaming category to group_label for each dataframe so that it's clear what each column represents
age = age.rename(columns = {"Category" : "group_label"})
