scf_combined.shape
scf_combined.head()
scf_combined.tail()
```

# Transformation Processes

For the transformation process, I filtered out the survey years 1989, 1992, 1995, 1998, and 2001 from the combined data frame. I did this because education loan balances from those years
are close to zero and are not relevant to the modern student debt crisis that this project is focused on. Keeping only 2004 and beyond makes the analysis more meaningful and relevant to
current and recent borrowers.

```{python}
# years that I do not want to conduct analysis on
years_to_exclude = [1989, 1992, 1995, 1998, 2001]
# transforming the data, so that the years to exclude are not in the final, combined dataframe that I'll use for analysis
scf_combined = scf_combined[~scf_combined["year"].isin(years_to_exclude)]
# also reset the index so that the index starts at 0 again
scf_combined = scf_combined.reset_index(drop=True)
```

# Analysis & Evaluation

**Descriptive Statistics and Visualizations**
Looking at the data overall, there are no missing values across any of the variables which makes sense since the SCF uses a process to fill in any missing responses. The descriptive stats
show that education loan balances vary a lot across different demographic groups and have grown significantly over time. The correlation between education loans and net worth shows a
positive relationship, meaning groups with higher education debt also tend to have higher net worth, which seems counterintuitive but makes sense at the group level since college educated
households borrow more but also earn more over time. The line chart shows that households under 35 carry the highest education loan balances across all years, which is expected since they
are the closest to their borrowing years and have had the least time to pay down their debt. The bar chart shows that in 2022, education loan balances are similar across racial groups in
dollar amounts, however, the burden is much heavier for black households compared to their total net worth.

**Bias and Unbalanced Patterns**
One of the biggest limitations of this data is that all the values are group level medians and not from individual households. The values only represent the median of a group of people 
and not for a single person. You have to be careful about making any strong and concrete conclusions from this data. Looking at racial disparities, black households carry education debt 
that makes up a much bigger share of their total net worth compared to other groups. This shows that even when loan amounts look similar across groups, the financial burden is not the 
same for everyone.

```{python}
# getting the descriptive stats for all the numeric variables in the combined dataframe
scf_combined.describe()
